whenever there's violent conflict  anywhere in the world the geopolitical  chess match that ensues ends up leading  to uncertainty and chaos that's exactly  what we're seeing in russia ukraine  conflict now of course what we  understand is that nation-states have  come together and they're levying these  financial sanctions against russia  whether it's the banks whether it's the  central bank or it's the everyday  citizens or it's the leadership of the  country they're all getting hit with  economic sanctions but we have to  understand that these decisions are  there to optimize for short-term  mitigation of risk rather than looking  at the long-term problems that they  create and so ultimately economist steve  hank he hit a great point where he said  weaponizing the swift international  payment system might cut russia off but  risks eroding the dollar-denominated  global financial system indeed it will  give rise to alternative systems  developed by china and russia just  another example of why sanctions are  counterproductive now first let's unpack  exactly what these sanctions are doing  they're kicking russian banks out of the  global financial system it's not  allowing the leadership nor the average  citizen to go ahead and transact with  various other countries to buy goods and  services on the open market or to access  their wealth that sits outside of russia  or china now that sounds like a great  idea it sounds like it'll serve as a  deterrent it'll inflict maximum pain on  the russian citizens and hopefully get  them to pressure putin to make different  decisions or to remove him as a leader  but what it does is it also puts every  single country in the world on notice if  you use the us dollar system the health  and sovereignty of your financial system  is dependent on having a good  relationship with the united states if  all of a sudden the united states with  the click of a button can remove you  from the global financial system if they  can pressure their allies into doing the  exact same thing what we are going to  watch is countries around the world  starting to develop insurance policies  or backup plans they're not necessarily  going to run and just buy bitcoin the  decentralized digital currency that may  be one thing that they do but they're  also going to go ahead and they're going  to create non-us dollar denominated  trading accounts with their trading  partners they're going to go ahead and  start to develop central bank digital  currencies that operate on payment rails  that aren't reliant on swift or the us  dollar system there's going to be a  variety of options that are pursued but  ultimately these types of decisions that  inflict maximum pain in the short term  they try to mitigate the short-term  risks everything from the invasion of  ukraine all the way to potential nuclear  war they're being celebrated today as a  way to address the problems we're facing  but they're creating long-term problems  it makes the us dollar system much more  risky and less sustainable over a long  time horizon and so that's fine to  address the short-term problems many  people including myself would say what  we're doing is actually going to have an  impact today we just have to be aware of  the long-term problems that we may be  creating maybe we can handle it maybe we  can address those long-term problems in  the future or maybe not i don't know but  what i do know is that being aware of  optimizing for short-term risk  mitigation while also creating long-term  problems is something we have to be well  aware of today
 

